    "answer": {
      "short": "$mn-m-n$.\n\nUse $mx+ny=N$ with $0\\le y<m$: an amount is unattainable precisely when its corresponding integer $x$ is negative. The largest occurs at $x=-1$, $y=m-1$.",
      "worked": "An amount $N$ is obtainable exactly when $mx+ny=N$ has integer solutions with $x,y\\ge0$. Since $\\operatorname{GCD}(m,n)=1$, every integer $N$ has an integer solution. All solutions from one particular solution $(x_0,y_0)$ are\n$$x=x_0+tn,\\qquad y=y_0-tm,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb{Z}.$$\nChoose the particular solution so that $0\\le y_0\\le m-1$.\n\nIf $x_0\\ge0$, this solution obtains $N$ using nonnegative numbers of coins. If $x_0<0$, every solution with $t\\le0$ has $x<0$, while every solution with $t\\ge1$ has $y\\le(m-1)-m<0$. Thus $N$ is unattainable exactly when $x_0<0$.\n\nFor an unattainable amount, $x_0\\le-1$ and $y_0\\le m-1$, so\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=mx_0+ny_0\\\\N&\\le-m+n(m-1)\\\\N&\\le mn-m-n.\\end{align*}$$\nEquality occurs for $x_0=-1$ and $y_0=m-1$, which has no nonnegative solution by the same argument. Since the coprime denominations exceed $1$, $mn-m-n$ is positive. Therefore the largest unattainable amount is $mn-m-n$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 124
